For the OP to clarify; Nethack can be configured using a configuration file
with an own syntax where all the options described in the Guidebook can be
set and easily distinguished, each option in a single line or more than one
if convenient. Given the huge amount of options that should be the preferred
way. And that way each user has it's own application specific config file.

Alternatively you can define a specific environment variable NETHACKOPTIONS;
this *could* make sense if you have just a handfull of options to set, and it
gets a nightmare if you try to stuff s huge set of options into that variable.
The bad consequences with environment variables are, that if you want them be
active for all Nethack sessions you have to put them somewhere externally;
Unix shells (and other OSes) each with their own syntax allow that to be
accomplished by the _shell specific_ configuration file (as opposed to the
application specific configuration file), thus cluttering up the environment.
This cluttering is worse if you are stupid enough to put that env variable
definition in the system wide shell configuration file which would make that
trash visible for everyone in the system. (This is still bad even if you're
the only one on the system.) Additional problems are that environment
variables can be accidentally redefined (cleared, changed) in the current
session without noticing.

To summarize; don't use shell config files for application config data.

